  "limitations": [
    "The research is currently at a pre-piloting Stage.",
    "The number of experiments is limited.",
    "The method of data registration is still being formulated in order to become more efficient.",
    "The EPOC EEG headset would at times not stream data, which then led to repeating the experiment or having a partial data set.",
    "Recording methods were not very accurate when it came to the synchronization of the sound files with the EEG recordings with a divergence of a couple of seconds.",
    "A Wilcoxon signed rank test yielded inconclusive conclusions.",
    "A bigger sample size is needed for more valid statistical results.",
    "Discrepancies in participant arousal levels could be due to random events or environmental factors (e.g., cold temperatures).",
    "Some aspects of “distance hearing” were missed in the analysis.",
    "Familiarity with audio signals is necessary for correctly determining the distance between sound source and auditory event.",
    "Auditory distance perception depends on loudness only for unfamiliar sound sources beyond 3m, potentially leading to misinterpretation of sound scene (proximity confused with loudness).",
    "Participants listening to binaural recordings without visual cues tend to localize the sound scene behind them.",
    "Signal processing might have yielded a sense of unfamiliarity to participants, rather than altering distance perception.",
    "The experimentation was only a pre-pilot study.",
    "It is difficult to assume that all people react emotionally exactly the same way, highlighting the need to customize affective interfaces to specific individual metrics.",
    "Future developments should include questionnaires and machine-learning algorithms to understand urban sounds that reduce/increase frustration and calibrate the application to individual needs."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This pre-pilot empirical study investigated the relationship between urban soundscapes and pedestrians' emotional states, specifically frustration, using portable EEG technology. Findings from Stage 1 indicated that closer proximity to sound sources generated higher frustration levels. In Stage 2, a responsive brain-controlled application was developed to adjust the perceived distance of sound sources in real-time based on a listener's frustration. The results showed that this application generally decreased frustration levels, suggesting its potential to enhance aural experience in urban environments. Limitations include the small sample size, preliminary nature of the study, and challenges in EEG data synchronization and sound localization.",
